course: dictionary
lesson: 6982
title: Pantomime in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the words around it tell you which one the writer means. «Pantomime, or panto, is a British Christmas show where the audience is meant to shout, boo the villain and cheer; most other plays expect a quiet audience.» The clues are Christmas show, shout and cheer, so here a pantomime is a funny play.
explain: If the sentence talks about actors using faces and hands but no words, pantomime means telling a story without speaking. If the sentence describes a silly, over-the-top mess, the word is being used informally.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The meeting was a play» does not fit the feeling of the sentence, but «The meeting was a silly mess» does, so the informal meaning is right there.
fact: Pantomime means a funny family play, usually at Christmas, based on a fairy tale.
fact: «Panto» is the short, informal name for a pantomime, used in the UK, Ireland and Australia.
fact: In a panto, a man dressed as a woman, called the Dame, makes the audience laugh.
fact: The audience shouts back, such as «He's behind you!» and «Oh no it isn't!»
fact: «Pantomime» can also mean telling a story with movements and faces but no words, which is called mime.
exercise: The audience shouts «Oh no it isn't!» at a Christmas show based on a fairy tale. What kind of show is it? | A football match | A silent film | *A pantomime | A science lecture
exercise: «The meeting was a pantomime.» What does pantomime mean here? | A quiet meeting with no talking | *A silly, over-the-top mess | A Christmas play with songs | A story told with mime only
exercise: Which word means telling a story with movements and faces but no words? | Dame | Villain | Cinderella | *Pantomime
test: «The Dame in the pantomime made the audience laugh.» Who is the Dame? | *A man dressed as a woman | A ghost who sings | A train driver | A princess in a castle
test: What is the audience doing when it shouts «Oh yes it is!» at a pantomime? | Asking for a refund | Booing the villain | *Joining in with the play | Falling asleep
exam: Which sentence uses pantomime in its informal sense? | We watched a pantomime about Aladdin at the theatre | The mime artist gave a pantomime with no words | *Our school trip was a pantomime of lost bags and missed buses | A pantomime is on at Christmas
